target/arm: 16 KiB target pages (-M ...,tomacco-page-bits) and a 16 KiB-only CPU.

tomacco-vphone states no minimum_page_bits, so machine_memory_init() commits
Arm's legacy default before any CPU exists: the engine runs with
1 KiB target pages (target_page.bits == 10, read from a paused
engine). The CPUs' later preference for 4 KiB cannot raise
it. Every structure that is per target page is then per KiB for a guest
whose XNU, SPTM, TXM and SEP firmware map only 16 KiB pages:

  - a TB ends at a 1 KiB boundary and goto_tb chains only within one, so a
    direct branch into the next KiB leaves through lookup_and_goto_ptr;
  - a 16 KiB guest page needs 16 softmmu TLB fills, and lg_page_size 14 >
    TARGET_PAGE_BITS makes every one a "large page": tlb_add_large_page
    merges them into one region per mmu_idx, so nearly every TLBI by VA
    (XNU unmap, protect, COW) becomes a full flush of that mmu_idx.

The machine option tomacco-page-bits=N (10, 12 or 14; any machine; 10 is
today's tomacco-vphone size, for A/B on another board) is read in
qemu_create_machine() ahead of the board's minimum_page_bits and is the
first preference, so it is the size in effect; the machine property of the
same name reads it back (and only confirms it when applied).

The CPU property tomacco-granule-16k (default off) reports and implements
only the 16 KiB granule: ID_AA64MMFR0 TGRAN4 = TGRAN64 = 0xf, TGRAN4_2 =
TGRAN64_2 = 1, TGRAN16/TGRAN16_2 kept (implemented); a guest that still
selects 4 or 64 KiB is translated with 16 KiB (the IMPDEF choice for an
unimplemented granule), logged once as a guest error. Only such a CPU
accepts 14-bit target pages; any other v8 CPU keeps asking for 12 bits and
refuses to realize on a 16 KiB machine. That is required, not cosmetic: a
translation smaller than the target page is right only where VA and PA
agree modulo the target page (tlb_set_page_full maps the whole target page
from the walk's aligned PA; fine for PMSA, wrong for 4 KiB page tables:
tests/engine/jump-cache-probe.S runs wrong code at 14 bits without it).

Every guest stage was checked for its granule: the SEP firmware writes
TCR_EL1 = 0x010000036520a59c (TG0 = TG1 = 16 KiB) and otherwise only
T0SZ/EPD0; SPTM writes 0x000800226511a511 (TG0 = TG1 = 16 KiB) and a
per-geometry TCR on address-space switch; TXM writes no TCR; XNU's set_tcr
has no caller and it reads ID_AA64MMFR0 only for ECV (bits 63:60). So the
AP cores and the SEP core (a separate "max" CPU, but the target page size
is one per process) can all be 16 KiB-only.

Run with run_ios_paravirt.py --target-page 16k (tomacco-page-bits=14 plus
-global max-arm-cpu.tomacco-granule-16k=on, which reaches the SEP CPU too)
or 4k (tomacco-page-bits=12 alone); an engine without this patch refuses the
machine option. Orthogonal to the other engine patches; with
qemu-tcg-jump-cache the jump cache keeps 512 entries per page bucket, now
per 16 KiB page.

